docs/measurement.md:199:`scripts/bench.py` builds both in release mode, then times `extract --json` over Mantine and Chakra in the Storybook addon's configuration (dependencies loaded, cache on after the warm-up run, inherited props not collapsed) over corpora with their dependencies installed with the two builds interleaved and alternating which goes first. It prints the median wall time and peak RSS of each and their ratio. It stops with the setup command to run when a corpus is not installed, because a sparse fetch has no dependencies to load: run `scripts/corpora.py setup --corpus mantine --corpus chakra` first.
docs/measurement.md:201:The 1.10× and 1.20× thresholds come from a calibration made when the bench ran with `--no-cache --no-load-dependencies` (with nothing changed, the ratio stayed inside 1.04 for time, 95th percentile of 100 trials per corpus on CI and a laptop, and inside 1.12 for memory on CI runners). The bench now runs the addon's configuration (dependencies loaded, warm cache) and that calibration has not been repeated for it, so the noise band may differ. More than 1.10× in time or 1.20× in memory is a starting point for looking. Nothing enforces that. Run it before a resolver change goes up for review, and say in the pull request when a cost is intended.
docs/measurement.md:225:The settings are the Storybook addon's (`createDocgenProvider` in `packages/storybook/src/provider.ts`): dependency types loaded, `collapseInheritedProps: 'none'`, a lazy session, and the NAPI defaults for the rest (`htmlAttributes: 'full'`, the on-disk cache on). The CLI runs passed the same props settings (`--load-dependencies --collapse-inherited-props none --html-attributes full`) so that the structural and the checker run list the same props. The sessions left out the per-story look at the files (`ensureFresh`, a few milliseconds on a large project) and `warmUp`/`fillAfter`, both off in the addon by default.
docs/measurement.md:230:- First load for a session is one request at a time in a seeded order that is the same in every mode: start (create plus index), the first component, and the first 10 and 50 components asked. The first run started with an empty cache directory (cold), the others with the cache it left (warm).
docs/measurement.md:247:- **First load, checker off:** session start 47 to 149 ms, the first component 0 to 103 ms, 10 components 24 to 216 ms and 50 components 46 to 684 ms (Chakra is the high end), the whole process peaking at 198 to 446 MiB with Node and the binding. A cold and a warm cache show no consistent difference under this load.
docs/measurement.md:280:It was measured once, by hand, and the script is not kept: two builds (`10f1f072`, the base, and this branch), five corpora on the pinned commits, five interleaved runs, `--no-cache`, whole-process wall time and peak RSS from `/usr/bin/time -l`, load average 29 at the start, so read differences under about 20% as noise: the `none` runs, which execute the same code and print identical output in both builds, differ by up to 16% in a lazy session.

